Key reasoning:
- Broadcast cost is driven by the union of edges used, so a shared trunk (Steiner tree) beats per-dst shortest paths when destinations cluster.
- Trying multiple attachment orderings (natural, cost-sorted, reversed, nearest-first) hedges against greedy ordering mistakes cheaply.
- Reusing the same edge list for all partitions avoids redundant transfers.
- Adding the independent-paths candidate as a fallback guarantees we never do worse than plain shortest paths.

